Kairo Tests
===========

Run everything from the repo root:

    lit Tests -s

`lit`, not `llvm-lit` -- the LLVM-branded wrapper is not installed here, and
`python3 -m lit` does not work either (lit is a package, not a runnable module).
Install it with `pip install lit` if the command is missing. FileCheck is also
required and is found on PATH as `FileCheck` or `filecheck`; pass
`--param filecheck=/abs/path` if it lives somewhere unusual.

Add `-v` to see the output of failing tests, or `-a` to see it for every test.


WHICH COMPILER GETS TESTED

These test STAGE 1, the self-hosted compiler built from Compiler/**/*.k -- the
thing you are editing. lit resolves it in this order:

    --param kairo=/abs/path        explicit, always wins
    KAIRO_BIN=/abs/path            environment
    build/*/release/bin/kairo      default

Note the default is the RELEASE path only. The fast edit/test loop builds with
`kbld kairo --debug`, which lands in build/<triple>/debug/bin/kairo and will NOT
be found. Testing a debug build therefore means saying so:

    KAIRO_BIN=$PWD/build/arm64-apple-macosx/debug/bin/kairo lit Tests -s

lit deliberately REFUSES to fall back to PATH. A bare `kairo` on PATH is stage
0, the prebuilt C++ transpiler that acts as the build driver -- it will answer,
lack the flags these tests use, and quietly test the wrong compiler. A missing
binary is a loud error here rather than a silent pass.

THE DEFAULT PATH IS OFTEN STALE. Nothing rebuilds build/*/release/bin/kairo
unless you ask for a release build, so a bare `lit Tests` will happily test a
binary from weeks ago and report results that have nothing to do with your
working tree. That staleness is sometimes deliberate -- an old release binary is
the A/B baseline for judging whether current sources changed parser behaviour --
which is all the more reason not to hit it by accident. If you just built, name
your binary.


READING THE RESULTS

The suite does not currently go green, and is not expected to. A fresh debug
build of the current tree gives:

    Total Discovered Tests: 250
      Passed           : 118
      Expectedly Failed:   1
      Unresolved       :  77
      Failed           :  54

A bare `lit Tests` against the stale release binary gives 107 passed / 65 failed
instead. Neither number is wrong; they are different compilers. Always know
which one you ran before quoting a figure.

The 54 failures are unimplemented language features -- alignof, closures,
f-string interpolation and similar -- not regressions. Before concluding that a
change broke something, get the failure LIST before and after and diff them; the
count alone is not informative, and neither is any individual failure without
that baseline.

All 77 UNRESOLVED are files with no RUN line: support files that other tests
import, and fixtures never converted to lit. lit reports them rather than
skipping them. They are noise, not breakage.


LAYOUT

    Parser/       parser and expression tests, the bulk of the suite
    Sema/         name resolution
    Imports/      module and FFI import cases, several multi-file
    PP/           preprocessor
    Codegen/      code emission; stage 1 cannot emit a binary yet, so these
                  currently pin the emitted token stream instead
    Clang/        clang interop
    HelloWorld/   end-to-end fixtures
    Bugs/         reduced reproducers
    Manual/       run by hand, not by lit

    NumericConformance/
                  NOT lit tests. Self-checking Kairo programs that assert their
                  own arithmetic and report via exit code, so they stay valid
                  the day stage 1 can emit a binary. A lit.local.cfg keeps lit
                  from collecting them. Run with:

                      ./Tests/NumericConformance/run.sh <path-to-kairo>

                  See Tests/NumericConformance/README -- it explains what those
                  programs cover and why numeric bugs get their own directory.


ADDING A TEST

Copy the RUN lines from a neighbouring file in the same directory; the shapes in
use are `--print-ast=tree`, `--print-tokens` and `--syntax-only`, each piped to
FileCheck. `%kairo` expands to the resolved binary plus `--error-format=basic`,
`%s` to the test file, `%t` to a scratch path.

Tests whose expected behaviour is not implemented yet get `// XFAIL: *` and a
comment saying what the CHECK lines describe and when to remove the marker --
see Parser/ExprParse/fstring_interpolation_unimplemented.k for the pattern. An
XFAIL that starts XPASSing means the feature landed.
